# In a regression of y on x, the OLS slope is B1hat = 0.03. Every value of x is then divided by 100 (for example, percent to decimal when the divisor is 100). What is the new OLS slope? (round to the hundredths)
B1hat <- 0.03; k <- 100               # INPUT (k = what x is divided by)
B1hat * k                             # check: 3
#   if x is MULTIPLIED by k instead:  B1hat / k


# A log-level regression gives log(y)hat = 0.6 + 0.07x. By about what percent does y change when x rises by 3? (Give a percent, e.g. 12.34 for 12.34%.) (round to the hundredths)
B1hat <- 0.07; dx <- 3                # INPUT
100 * B1hat * dx                      # check: 21


# A level-log regression gives yhat = 14 + 6.1 log(x). By about how many units does y change when x rises by 12%? (round to the hundredths)
B1hat <- 6.1; pct_dx <- 12            # INPUT
(B1hat / 100) * pct_dx                # check: 0.73


